window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA.python = {
  notes: [
    { title: "What Python is", body: "Python is a programming language. You write instructions in a file that ends with .py. The computer reads that file from the top and follows each line. The words look closer to English than many other languages. Spaces at the start of a line are part of the grammar, not just pretty layout. People use Python for websites, small scripts, data tables, and machine learning. Start with tiny programs that print values so you can see what happened." },
    { title: "Variables", body: "A variable is a name stuck on a value. You write name = value, with no let or const. Later you can point the same name at a new value. Use snake_case names like user_name, not userName. The name is a label; the value is what lives in memory. Print the name when you want to see the value. A common mix-up is thinking the name is the text itself, not a label for it." },
    { title: "Types", body: "A type is the kind of thing a value is. int is a whole number. float is a number with a decimal point. str is text. bool is True or False. None means empty on purpose. list, tuple, dict, and set hold other values. type(x) shows the type. Convert with int(), str(), and float() when you need a different kind." },
    { title: "Lists", body: "A list is a row of items in square brackets. The first item is at index 0. You can append, insert, or replace items because a list can change. fruits[0] reads the first item. fruits[-1] reads the last item. len(fruits) counts how many items you have. Keep related items in one list instead of making fruit1, fruit2, fruit3." },
    { title: "Dict", body: "A dictionary stores values under names called keys. You write user = {'name': 'Ada'}. Read with user['name'] or user.get('name'). Keys are usually strings. Lookup by key is fast. Use get when the key might be missing, because square brackets raise KeyError. A dict is a labeled form, not a numbered list." },
    { title: "if / for / while", body: "if picks a path when a condition is True. elif is the next test. else runs when nothing above matched. for item in items visits each item. range(5) is 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. while repeats until the condition becomes False. Indent the body four spaces. A line back at the left edge is not inside the if or the loop." },
    { title: "Functions", body: "A function is a named recipe you can reuse. def greet(name): starts it. The indented lines are the body. return sends a value back to the caller. Inputs in the brackets are parameters. You call it with greet('Ada'). Default arguments fill in when the caller skips a value. Keep one job per function so it stays easy to test." },
    { title: "Files", body: "You open a file with open and a path. with open(...) as f: closes the file even if something fails. f.read() pulls the whole text. encoding='utf-8' keeps letters correct. Mode 'w' overwrites. Mode 'a' appends. Do not leave files open by hand if you can use with. Read line by line when the file is huge." },
    { title: "Modules", body: "A module is a .py file full of useful names. import math loads that file. Then you use math.sqrt. from datetime import date brings one name into your file. pip installs extra packages from the internet. After install, you still write import. Your own helpers.py next to this file can be imported the same way." },
    { title: "Errors", body: "try runs the risky lines. except runs if that kind of error happens. Catch a specific error like ValueError, not every error in the world. The program can print a kind message and keep going. raise starts an error on purpose when input is illegal. Bare except: hides bugs you need to see. Read the error type; it tells you what went wrong." },
    { title: "venv", body: "A virtual environment is a private folder of packages for one project. Project A can use pandas 2 while project B uses pandas 1. They do not fight. You create it once, activate it, then pip install inside it. Put requirements.txt in Git, not the venv folder. The import lines in your .py files stay the same. Isolating packages stops 'it works on my laptop' fights." },
    { title: "Interview habit", body: "In an interview, talk while you type. Say list versus tuple, and mutable versus immutable, in one clear sentence. Start with a tiny example, then name an empty input. Prefer a readable loop over a clever one-liner. Mention how you would read a CSV or a JSON body. Keep names honest. A small working function beats a half-remembered trick." }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, level: "beginner", q: "What is Python?", a: "Python is a programming language. You tell the computer what to do by writing lines in a file that ends with .py. The computer reads those lines from top to bottom.\n\nThe words look close to English, so many beginners start here. People use it for websites, small scripts, data tables, and machine learning. Spaces at the start of a line are part of the language, not just style.\n\nIn the code: a line that starts with # is a comment, so Python skips it. message stores the text Hello from Python, then print(message) shows it. year stores 2026 and print(year) shows that number.\n\nA common mistake is never printing. If you only write year = 2026, you will not see the value.", code: "# a tiny Python program\n# a comment starts with #\n\nmessage = \"Hello from Python\"\nprint(message)\n\n# a number can live in a name too\nyear = 2026\nprint(year)" },
    { id: 2, level: "beginner", q: "How do you print something?", a: "print is a built-in function that shows a value on the screen. You put the value inside the round brackets. You can pass more than one value, separated by commas.\n\nPython puts a space between those values by default. Beginners use print to check what a name currently holds. That is how you watch the program think.\n\nIn the code: name holds Nitin and age holds 21. print(\"Hello\") shows one word. print(name, age) shows both values. print(\"My name is\", name) builds a short sentence.\n\nA common mistake is writing print name without brackets. In Python 3 that is a syntax error.", code: "name = \"Nitin\"\nage = 21\n\n# print one value\nprint(\"Hello\")\n\n# print many values, separated by a space\nprint(name, age)\n\n# print a full sentence\nprint(\"My name is\", name)" },
    { id: 3, level: "beginner", q: "How do you make a variable?", a: "A variable is a label stuck on a value. You write the name, then =, then the value. Python has no let or const.\n\nYou can later point the same name at a new value. The old number is no longer used by that name. Choose clear snake_case names.\n\nIn the code: name stores the text Nitin. age starts as 20, then age = 21 points the same name at 21. print shows each value.\n\nA common mistake is thinking = compares two values. In Python, = stores. == is the comparison.", code: "# make a text value\nname = \"Nitin\"\n\n# make a number value\nage = 20\n\n# point the same name at a new value\nage = 21\n\nprint(name)\nprint(age)" },
    { id: 4, level: "beginner", q: "What are the basic types?", a: "A type is the kind of thing a value is. int is a whole number. float has a decimal point. str is text. bool is True or False. None means empty on purpose.\n\nLists, tuples, dicts, and sets hold other values. type(x) prints the type name. Convert with int(), str(), and float() when you need a different kind.\n\nIn the code: age is an int, price is a float, name is a str, ok is a bool, and empty is None. type(age), type(name), and type(ok) print those kinds.\n\nA common mistake is mixing types in math, like \"21\" + 1. Turn the text into int first.", code: "# whole number, decimal, text, yes/no, empty\nage = 21                 # int\nprice = 19.5             # float\nname = \"Ada\"             # str\nok = True                # bool\nempty = None             # None\n\nprint(type(age))\nprint(type(name))\nprint(type(ok))" },
    { id: 5, level: "beginner", q: "How do you write a comment?", a: "A comment is a note for humans. The computer skips it. A line that starts with # is a comment. You can also put # after some code on the same line.\n\nA triple-quoted string at the start of a function is a docstring. Editors and help() show that docstring. Comments should explain why, not repeat the line in English.\n\nIn the code: the first line is a full-line comment. score = 80 has a side comment. add has a docstring, then return a + b. print(add(2, 3)) shows 5.\n\nA common mistake is commenting out broken code forever instead of deleting it. Dead comments go stale and lie.", code: "# this whole line is a note for you\n\nscore = 80  # this note sits beside the code\n\ndef add(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Return a plus b. This is a docstring.\"\"\"\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(2, 3))" },
    { id: 6, level: "beginner", q: "Why does indentation matter?", a: "Python uses spaces at the start of a line to show which lines belong together. if, for, def, and else all need an indented body. Wrong spaces cause a SyntaxError or a logic bug.\n\nUse 4 spaces each time you go one level in. Do not mix tabs and spaces. A line back at the left edge is not inside the if.\n\nIn the code: marks is 75, so the if body runs. Both Pass and Good work print because they are indented under if. Done is not indented, so it always prints after the choice.\n\nA common mistake is indenting Done under if. Then Done would skip on Fail.", code: "marks = 75\n\n# the print line belongs to the if, so it is indented\nif marks >= 40:\n    print(\"Pass\")\n    print(\"Good work\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Fail\")\n\n# this line is back at the left, so it always runs\nprint(\"Done\")" },
    { id: 7, level: "beginner", q: "How do you take user input?", a: "input() shows a message and waits for the person to type. Whatever they type comes back as text, even if they type 21. If you need a number, wrap the text with int().\n\nint fails if the text is not a number, so later you catch ValueError. The teaching snippet pretends the user already typed, so you can see the types clearly.\n\nIn the code: name is the text Ada, then print greets that name. age_text is the string 21. int(age_text) makes a real number, so age + 1 is 22.\n\nA common mistake is adding 1 to the raw input string. \"21\" + 1 crashes.", code: "# input always gives text (a str)\nname = \"Ada\"          # pretend the user typed this\nprint(\"Hello\", name)\n\n# turn text into a whole number\nage_text = \"21\"\nage = int(age_text)\nprint(age + 1)        # 22, math works on int" },
    { id: 8, level: "beginner", q: "f-string?", a: "An f-string is text with an f in front of the quotes. You put a name or a small expression inside curly braces. Python fills in the value when it builds the string.\n\nIt is easier to read than gluing pieces with +. You need Python 3.6 or newer. Keep the braces simple so the line stays clear.\n\nIn the code: name is Nitin and score is 95. The f-string fills both into one sentence. The last print computes 20 + 1 inside the braces and shows 21.\n\nA common mistake is forgetting the f. Then {name} prints as those characters, not the value.", code: "name = \"Nitin\"\nscore = 95\n\n# f means: fill the braces with values\ntext = f\"Hello {name}, you scored {score}\"\nprint(text)\n\n# you can even put a little expression inside\nprint(f\"Next year you will be {20 + 1}\")" },
    { id: 9, level: "beginner", q: "list vs tuple?", a: "A list is a row of items you can change. A tuple is also a row, but you cannot change the items after you make it. Lists use square brackets. Tuples use round brackets.\n\nUse a list when you will add or remove items. Use a tuple for a fixed record, like an (x, y) point. Index 0 is still the first item in both.\n\nIn the code: fruits starts with apple and mango, then append adds banana. point is the tuple (10, 20). print(point[0]) shows 10. The commented line would fail because tuples cannot assign to an index.\n\nA common mistake is using a list for a pair that should never change, then accidentally appending to it.", code: "# list: can change\nfruits = [\"apple\", \"mango\"]\nfruits.append(\"banana\")\nprint(fruits)\n\n# tuple: cannot change the items\npoint = (10, 20)\nprint(point[0])\n# point[0] = 99  would fail" },
    { id: 10, level: "beginner", q: "How do you add to a list?", a: "append puts a new item at the end of a list. insert puts an item at a chosen index. The + operator glues two lists into a new list.\n\nThe original list stays if you use +, unless you assign back. Index 0 is the front. These methods change or build lists; they do not print by themselves.\n\nIn the code: fruits starts as apple, mango. append adds banana at the end. insert(0, \"kiwi\") puts kiwi at the front. more is a new list with orange added, then print shows that new list.\n\nA common mistake is writing fruits.append(\"orange\") and expecting a new list back. append returns None.", code: "fruits = [\"apple\", \"mango\"]\n\n# add at the end\nfruits.append(\"banana\")\n\n# add at index 0 (the front)\nfruits.insert(0, \"kiwi\")\n\n# glue two lists into a new list\nmore = fruits + [\"orange\"]\nprint(more)" },
    { id: 11, level: "beginner", q: "How do you loop a list?", a: "A for loop visits each item in a list, one at a time. The loop body is indented. You do not have to write i = 0 yourself.\n\nIf you also need the position, wrap the list with enumerate. enumerate gives a count and the item together. The count starts at 0 unless you pass start=.\n\nIn the code: fruits has three names. The first loop prints apple, mango, banana. The second loop prints 0 apple, then 1 mango, then 2 banana.\n\nA common mistake is looping range(len(fruits)) when you only need the item. for fruit in fruits is clearer.", code: "fruits = [\"apple\", \"mango\", \"banana\"]\n\n# visit each item\nfor fruit in fruits:\n    print(fruit)\n\n# visit with a count (0, 1, 2)\nfor i, fruit in enumerate(fruits):\n    print(i, fruit)" },
    { id: 12, level: "beginner", q: "What is range?", a: "range makes a sequence of whole numbers. It is used when you need a counter, not a ready-made list of names. The end number is not included.\n\nrange(5) is 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. range(1, 5) starts at 1. The third argument is the step. list(range(...)) turns it into a real list so you can print it all at once.\n\nIn the code: the for loop prints 0 through 4. list(range(1, 5)) is [1, 2, 3, 4]. list(range(0, 10, 2)) is the even numbers up to 8.\n\nA common mistake is expecting range(5) to include 5. The stop value is always left out.", code: "# 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\nfor n in range(5):\n    print(n)\n\n# 1 to 4\nprint(list(range(1, 5)))\n\n# 0, 2, 4, 6, 8\nprint(list(range(0, 10, 2)))" },
    { id: 13, level: "beginner", q: "if / elif / else?", a: "if checks the first rule. elif means else if, for the next rule. else runs when no rule above was true. Only one of those blocks runs.\n\nThe condition must be True or False. Order matters: the first match wins. Put the tightest or most important test in a place that cannot be shadowed by an earlier match.\n\nIn the code: marks is 75. 75 >= 40 is True, so result becomes pass. The elif never runs. print shows pass.\n\nA common mistake is writing the 20 check first. Then 75 would match retry if you ordered the tests backwards.", code: "marks = 75\n\nif marks >= 40:\n    result = \"pass\"\nelif marks >= 20:\n    result = \"retry\"\nelse:\n    result = \"fail\"\n\nprint(result)" },
    { id: 14, level: "beginner", q: "and / or / not?", a: "and is True only if both sides are True. or is True if at least one side is True. not flips True to False and False to True.\n\nThese words combine yes/no checks into one rule. Use extra brackets when you mix them so you can read the rule. Keep each side a clear comparison.\n\nIn the code: age is 20 and has_ticket is True, so both sides of and pass and Come in prints. True or False is True. not True is False.\n\nA common mistake is writing if age >= 18 or has_ticket when you meant both must be true. Then a child with a ticket would get in.", code: "age = 20\nhas_ticket = True\n\n# both must be true\nif age >= 18 and has_ticket:\n    print(\"Come in\")\n\n# at least one is true\nprint(True or False)   # True\nprint(not True)        # False" },
    { id: 15, level: "beginner", q: "== vs is?", a: "== asks whether two values look the same. is asks whether two names point at the exact same object in memory. For numbers and text, use ==.\n\nFor None, the usual style is x is None. Two lists with the same items can be equal but not the same object. Assignment like c = a does not copy; it shares.\n\nIn the code: a and b are two lists that both contain 1, 2, so a == b is True and a is b is False. c is a, so a is c is True. x is None prints True.\n\nA common mistake is using is to compare lists or strings you built separately. That can be False even when == is True.", code: "a = [1, 2]\nb = [1, 2]\nc = a\n\nprint(a == b)   # True, same items\nprint(a is b)   # False, two different lists\nprint(a is c)   # True, same list object\n\nx = None\nprint(x is None)" },
    { id: 16, level: "beginner", q: "What is None?", a: "None is Python's empty value. It means nothing here. It is not the number 0 and not the empty string.\n\nA function that does not return anything actually returns None. Check missing data with is None. Do not use == None in new code; is None is the style.\n\nIn the code: silent prints a message but has no return, so result is None. box is set to None on purpose. if box is None prints empty box.\n\nA common mistake is treating None like 0 in math. None + 1 crashes.", code: "def silent():\n    print(\"I do not return a value\")\n\nresult = silent()\nprint(result)          # None\n\nbox = None\nif box is None:\n    print(\"empty box\")" },
    { id: 17, level: "beginner", q: "How do you write a function?", a: "A function is a named recipe with inputs and maybe an output. def starts it. Names in the brackets are parameters. The indented lines are the body.\n\nreturn sends a value back to the caller. After return, the function stops. You use the function by writing its name and brackets with values inside.\n\nIn the code: add takes a and b, stores a + b in total, and returns total. add(2, 3) puts 2 in a and 3 in b. answer is 5, then print shows 5.\n\nA common mistake is forgetting return. Then the caller gets None even if you computed the right number.", code: "def add(a, b):\n    # a and b are inputs\n    total = a + b\n    return total      # send the answer back\n\n# use (call) the function\nanswer = add(2, 3)\nprint(answer)         # 5" },
    { id: 18, level: "beginner", q: "default argument?", a: "A default argument is a value used when the caller skips that input. You write it as name=\"friend\" on the def line. If the caller passes a name, that name wins.\n\nDo not use a mutable list as a default. That list is created once and shared. The safe pattern is items=None, then make a new list inside.\n\nIn the code: greet() uses friend and prints Hi friend. greet(\"Ada\") uses Ada. add_item makes a fresh list when items is None, then appends.\n\nA common mistake is def f(items=[]) which leaks leftover items into the next call.", code: "def greet(name=\"friend\"):\n    return \"Hi \" + name\n\nprint(greet())         # Hi friend\nprint(greet(\"Ada\"))    # Hi Ada\n\n# safe empty list default\ndef add_item(item, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(item)\n    return items" },
    { id: 19, level: "intermediate", q: "Why not def f(items=[])?", a: "Python builds a default argument value only once, when the function is defined. If that value is a list, every call that skips the argument shares the same list.\n\nLeftovers from the last call then show up next time. The fix is items=None, then create a new list inside the function. That gives each call its own bag.\n\nIn the code: bad(\"a\") returns ['a']. bad(\"b\") returns ['a', 'b'] because the default list still held a. good uses None and would not keep leftovers across calls.\n\nA common mistake is thinking each call gets a fresh [] from the def line. It does not.", code: "def bad(item, items=[]):\n    items.append(item)\n    return items\n\nprint(bad(\"a\"))   # ['a']\nprint(bad(\"b\"))   # ['a', 'b']  leftover from last call\n\ndef good(item, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(item)\n    return items" },
    { id: 20, level: "beginner", q: "What is a dictionary?", a: "A dictionary stores values under keys. It looks like {\"name\": \"Ada\"}. Lookup by key is fast. Keys must be immutable, like str or int.\n\nYou read with square brackets. You can add or change a field with assignment. print of a dict shows the current keys and values.\n\nIn the code: user has id, name, and city. user[\"name\"] is Ada. user[\"city\"] = \"Delhi\" overwrites Pune. print(user) shows the updated dict.\n\nA common mistake is user[\"email\"] when email was never stored. That raises KeyError.", code: "user = {\n    \"id\": 1,\n    \"name\": \"Ada\",\n    \"city\": \"Pune\"\n}\n\nprint(user[\"name\"])     # Ada\nuser[\"city\"] = \"Delhi\"  # change one field\nprint(user)" },
    { id: 21, level: "beginner", q: "How do you read a dict safely?", a: "user[\"email\"] raises KeyError if the key is missing. .get(\"email\") returns None instead. You can pass a default: .get(\"email\", \"none\").\n\nSafe reads matter when data comes from a file or the web. Square brackets are fine when the key must exist. Prefer get when it might not.\n\nIn the code: user only has name. get(\"email\") prints None. get(\"email\", \"none\") prints none. user[\"name\"] is safe and prints Ada.\n\nA common mistake is using get and then forgetting that None means missing, not the text \"None\".", code: "user = {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\n\n# safe: missing key becomes None\nprint(user.get(\"email\"))\n\n# safe with a default\nprint(user.get(\"email\", \"none\"))\n\n# this would crash: user[\"email\"]\nprint(user[\"name\"])" },
    { id: 22, level: "beginner", q: "How do you loop a dict?", a: ".items() gives you key and value pairs. .keys() is only keys. .values() is only values. The for loop body is indented like any other loop.\n\nYou walk the dict row by row. list() around keys or values makes a snapshot list you can print. Order in modern Python follows insertion order.\n\nIn the code: user has name Ada and age 21. The loop prints each key next to its value. Then the code prints the list of keys and the list of values.\n\nA common mistake is for key in user.items() without unpacking. Then key is a (name, value) tuple and the print looks wrong.", code: "user = {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"age\": 21}\n\nfor key, value in user.items():\n    print(key, value)\n\nprint(list(user.keys()))\nprint(list(user.values()))" },
    { id: 23, level: "beginner", q: "What is a set?", a: "A set is a bag of unique items. Duplicates disappear. Checking whether an item is in the set is fast. Sets are not lists, so do not use a set when order is the main story.\n\nYou write {1, 2, 3} for a non-empty set. add puts a new item in. in tests membership.\n\nIn the code: nums starts as {1, 2, 2, 3} and prints as {1, 2, 3}. 2 in nums is True. add(4) inserts 4.\n\nA common mistake is writing {} for an empty set. {} is an empty dict. Use set().", code: "nums = {1, 2, 2, 3}\nprint(nums)          # {1, 2, 3}\n\nprint(2 in nums)     # True, fast check\n\nnums.add(4)\nprint(nums)" },
    { id: 24, level: "beginner", q: "How do you remove duplicates from a list?", a: "set(items) drops duplicates, then list() turns it back into a list. That way may change order. To keep first-seen order, use dict.fromkeys, then list().\n\nPick the method that matches what you need. Unique tags often do not care about order. A guest list of first arrivals does.\n\nIn the code: items has a, b, a, c, b. list(set(items)) is unique but order is not promised. dict.fromkeys keeps a, then b, then c.\n\nA common mistake is assuming set() keeps the original order. Do not rely on that in interviews.", code: "items = [\"a\", \"b\", \"a\", \"c\", \"b\"]\n\n# unique, order not guaranteed\nprint(list(set(items)))\n\n# unique, keep first order: a, b, c\nprint(list(dict.fromkeys(items)))" },
    { id: 25, level: "beginner", q: "slicing?", a: "A slice cuts a piece of a list or string. The pattern is start:stop. Stop is not included. [:2] is the first two items. [-1] is the last item. [::-1] makes a reversed copy.\n\nSlicing does not change the original unless you assign back. It is a new list of the chosen items. Use it to peek at a window of data.\n\nIn the code: items is a through e. [1:4] is b, c, d. [:2] is a, b. [-1] is e. [::-1] is e down to a.\n\nA common mistake is thinking [1:4] includes index 4. Index 4 is the stop, so it is left out.", code: "items = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\", \"e\"]\n\nprint(items[1:4])    # b, c, d\nprint(items[:2])     # a, b\nprint(items[-1])     # e\nprint(items[::-1])   # reversed copy" },
    { id: 26, level: "beginner", q: "How do you join strings?", a: "join glues many strings into one string. The separator sits on the left: \", \".join(parts). Every item must already be text.\n\nIf you have numbers, turn each into str first. join is cleaner than a loop that adds commas by hand. The result is one string, not a list.\n\nIn the code: parts joins to a, b, c with comma-space. The number list uses a generator that calls str(n), then joins with a dash to make 1-2-3.\n\nA common mistake is writing parts.join(\", \"). join lives on the separator string, not on the list.", code: "parts = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\nprint(\", \".join(parts))     # a, b, c\n\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(\"-\".join(str(n) for n in nums))  # 1-2-3" },
    { id: 27, level: "beginner", q: "split a string?", a: "split cuts a string into a list of pieces. You pass the separator, like a comma. strip removes extra spaces on the ends of a piece.\n\nThis is how you read a simple CSV line. You often split, then strip each piece. The original string is not changed.\n\nIn the code: line is a, b, c. split(\",\") keeps the spaces, so parts has \"a\", \" b\", \" c\". The list comprehension strips those spaces. \"  hi  \".strip() becomes hi.\n\nA common mistake is using the split pieces without strip, then keys look like \" b\" and never match \"b\".", code: "line = \"a, b, c\"\nparts = line.split(\",\")\nprint(parts)\n\nclean = [p.strip() for p in parts]\nprint(clean)\n\nprint(\"  hi  \".strip())" },
    { id: 28, level: "beginner", q: "len, min, max, sum?", a: "len counts how many items. min finds the smallest. max finds the largest. sum adds numbers. They work on lists and many other collections.\n\nThese save you from writing small loops. They expect a non-empty list for min and max. len works on strings too, counting characters.\n\nIn the code: nums is 3, 1, 4, 1, 5. len is 5, min is 1, max is 5, sum is 14.\n\nA common mistake is calling sum on a list of strings. You must have numbers, or convert first.", code: "nums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]\n\nprint(len(nums))   # 5\nprint(min(nums))   # 1\nprint(max(nums))   # 5\nprint(sum(nums))   # 14" },
    { id: 29, level: "beginner", q: "How do you sort a list?", a: ".sort() changes the list in place and returns None. sorted() builds a new list and leaves the old one alone. key= tells Python what to sort by, like a field on a dict.\n\nUse sorted when you still need the original order later. Use sort when this list itself should change. Both default to ascending order.\n\nIn the code: sorted(nums) prints [1, 2, 3] while nums is still [3, 1, 2]. After nums.sort(), nums is [1, 2, 3]. The last line sorts users by the age field.\n\nA common mistake is writing nums = nums.sort(). Then nums becomes None.", code: "nums = [3, 1, 2]\nprint(sorted(nums))   # new list [1, 2, 3]\nprint(nums)           # still [3, 1, 2]\n\nnums.sort()\nprint(nums)           # now [1, 2, 3]\n\nusers = [{\"age\": 21}, {\"age\": 18}]\nprint(sorted(users, key=lambda u: u[\"age\"]))" },
    { id: 30, level: "beginner", q: "What is a lambda?", a: "A lambda is a tiny unnamed function in one line. You write lambda x: x * 2. It is handy as a key= for sort or max.\n\nIf the logic needs more than one line, write a real def. Keep lambdas small so they stay easy to read. They still take inputs and return a value.\n\nIn the code: double(5) is 10. users has Ada age 21 and Lin age 18. sorted with key=lambda u: u[\"age\"] puts Lin first, so [0][\"name\"] is Lin.\n\nA common mistake is putting a return inside a lambda. Lambdas only have the expression after the colon.", code: "double = lambda x: x * 2\nprint(double(5))      # 10\n\nusers = [{\"name\": \"Ada\", \"age\": 21}, {\"name\": \"Lin\", \"age\": 18}]\n# sort by the age field\nyoung_first = sorted(users, key=lambda u: u[\"age\"])\nprint(young_first[0][\"name\"])" },
    { id: 31, level: "beginner", q: "list comprehension?", a: "A list comprehension is a short way to build a new list from an old one. You can also filter with if. It is fine when the idea fits on one line.\n\nIf it gets hard to read, use a normal for loop. The old list stays unchanged. The result is a new list.\n\nIn the code: nums is 1 through 5. doubled is each n * 2, so 2, 4, 6, 8, 10. evens keeps n where n % 2 == 0, so 2 and 4.\n\nA common mistake is writing a comprehension that also prints or appends to another list. Keep it as a pure new list.", code: "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n\n# double each number\ndoubled = [n * 2 for n in nums]\n\n# keep only the even numbers\nevens = [n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0]\n\nprint(doubled)\nprint(evens)" },
    { id: 32, level: "beginner", q: "How do you open a file?", a: "open gives you a file object. with makes sure the file closes, even if something fails. read() pulls the whole text into a string. encoding=\"utf-8\" is the usual choice for text.\n\nFor a huge file, loop line by line instead of one giant read. The teaching snippet uses a string so you can see the loop without a real disk file. The commented lines show the real with open shape.\n\nIn the code: text is hello file. splitlines() makes a list of lines. The for loop prints each line. The commented with open would read data.txt the same way.\n\nA common mistake is calling open without with and forgetting to close. Locks and lost data show up later.", code: "# teaching: how with open looks\n# (the file name would be a real path on disk)\n\ntext = \"hello file\"\nlines = text.splitlines()\n\n# same idea as reading a file, line by line\nfor line in lines:\n    print(line)\n\n# with open(\"data.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n#     text = f.read()" },
    { id: 33, level: "beginner", q: "write a file?", a: "Mode w writes and overwrites. Mode a appends at the end. write puts a string into the file. with closes the file for you. encoding keeps letters correct.\n\nThe teaching snippet uses StringIO as a fake file in memory. That lets you see write and getvalue without touching disk. The commented with open is the real pattern.\n\nIn the code: page is an empty buffer. Two write calls add hello and second line, each ending with a newline. getvalue() prints both lines. The comment shows writing hello to out.txt.\n\nA common mistake is opening with w when you meant a. w wipes the old file.", code: "# teaching the write idea with a string buffer\nfrom io import StringIO\n\npage = StringIO()\npage.write(\"hello\n\")\npage.write(\"second line\n\")\nprint(page.getvalue())\n\n# with open(\"out.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n#     f.write(\"hello\")" },
    { id: 34, level: "beginner", q: "What is a module?", a: "A module is a .py file full of useful names. import math loads that file so you can use math.sqrt. from math import sqrt brings one name into your file.\n\nYou can also import your own file next to this one. Do not copy-paste the same helper into every file. The module is a toolbox you open when you need a tool.\n\nIn the code: import math lets you print math.pi and math.sqrt(9). from math import sqrt lets you call sqrt(16) with no prefix. The comment says helpers.py would be import helpers.\n\nA common mistake is from math import * which dumps many names and hides where they came from.", code: "import math\nfrom math import sqrt\n\nprint(math.pi)\nprint(math.sqrt(9))\nprint(sqrt(16))\n\n# your own file helpers.py would be: import helpers" },
    { id: 35, level: "beginner", q: "What is pip?", a: "pip is the tool that installs extra Python packages. Those packages live in a catalog called PyPI. After a package is installed, you import it in code.\n\nVersions go in requirements.txt so others get the same set. pip is the shop. import is using the item at home. json in the snippet is a built-in module, same import idea.\n\nIn the code: json.dumps turns a dict into text. json.loads turns that text back into a dict. print shows both steps.\n\nA common mistake is importing a name you never installed in this environment. You get ModuleNotFoundError.", code: "# after pandas is installed, you use it like this\n# import pandas as pd\n\n# many small packages are just import + a function\nimport json\n\ndata = {\"ok\": True}\ntext = json.dumps(data)\nprint(text)\nprint(json.loads(text))" },
    { id: 36, level: "beginner", q: "What is a virtual environment?", a: "A virtual environment is a private folder of packages for one project. Project A can have pandas 2 and project B can have pandas 1. They do not fight because each has its own folder.\n\nYou create it once per project, then install packages inside it. The import lines you write stay the same. Only the package folder changes.\n\nIn the code: project_a maps pandas to 2.0 and project_b maps pandas to 1.5. The prints show each project using its own version. The comment is the real import you would still write.\n\nA common mistake is installing into the global Python, then wondering why another project broke.", code: "# teaching picture of two isolated package folders\nproject_a = {\"pandas\": \"2.0\"}\nproject_b = {\"pandas\": \"1.5\"}\n\n# same import name, different versions, no clash\nprint(\"A uses\", project_a[\"pandas\"])\nprint(\"B uses\", project_b[\"pandas\"])\n\n# your code still looks like: import pandas as pd" },
    { id: 37, level: "beginner", q: "requirements.txt?", a: "requirements.txt is a text file that lists package names and versions. Another computer can install the same set from that list. You put this file in Git, not the whole venv folder.\n\nPinning versions makes it work on my machine less painful. Each line is usually name==version. The list is small; the installed packages stay local.\n\nIn the code: requirements holds two pinned lines. The loop splits each line on == and prints the name and version.\n\nA common mistake is committing the .venv folder instead of this short list. The folder is huge and machine-specific.", code: "# this is the idea of a requirements list\n# (a real file would sit next to your .py files)\n\nrequirements = [\n    \"pandas==2.2.0\",\n    \"scikit-learn==1.5.0\",\n]\n\nfor line in requirements:\n    name, version = line.split(\"==\")\n    print(name, \"pinned at\", version)" },
    { id: 38, level: "beginner", q: "How do you handle errors?", a: "try runs the risky lines. except runs if that kind of error happens. Catch a specific error like ValueError, not every error in the world.\n\nYou can print a kind message and ask again. Bare except: hides bugs you need to see. Read the error type; it names the problem.\n\nIn the code: text is 21, so int(text) works and prints number is 21. If text were hello, except ValueError would print that was not a number.\n\nA common mistake is except: with no type. Then even KeyboardInterrupt and typos get swallowed.", code: "text = \"21\"\n\ntry:\n    number = int(text)\n    print(\"number is\", number)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"that was not a number\")\n\n# catch the specific error, not every error" },
    { id: 39, level: "beginner", q: "What is a class in Python?", a: "A class is a blueprint for objects. __init__ runs when you make a new object. self is that new object. You store data as self.name and write methods under the class.\n\nobj = Dog(\"Rex\") makes one dog. Each object has its own data. Methods are functions that live on the class and receive self.\n\nIn the code: Dog.__init__ stores the name. bark returns a sentence using that name. rex = Dog(\"Rex\") then print(rex.name) and print(rex.bark()) show Rex and Rex says woof.\n\nA common mistake is forgetting self in a method. Then Python complains about missing arguments.", code: "class Dog:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def bark(self):\n        return self.name + \" says woof\"\n\nrex = Dog(\"Rex\")\nprint(rex.name)\nprint(rex.bark())" },
    { id: 40, level: "beginner", q: "What is self?", a: "self is the current object, the one before the dot. When you call rex.bark(), self is rex. You write self.name to read or store data on that object.\n\nYou do not pass self yourself; Python fills it in. Each object wears its own data. Two objects of the same class do not share self.name unless you designed that.\n\nIn the code: User stores name on self. hello reads self.name. ada and lin are two users. ada.hello() is Hi Ada. lin.hello() is Hi Lin.\n\nA common mistake is using a class variable when you meant self.name, so every user shares one name.", code: "class User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name     # store on THIS user\n\n    def hello(self):\n        return \"Hi \" + self.name\n\nada = User(\"Ada\")\nlin = User(\"Lin\")\nprint(ada.hello())\nprint(lin.hello())" },
    { id: 41, level: "intermediate", q: "mutable vs immutable?", a: "Immutable means you cannot edit the object; you make a new one. int, str, and tuple are immutable. Mutable means you can change the same object: list, dict, set.\n\nIf you pass a list into a function that appends, the caller sees the marks. Replacing a name with a new string does not change other names that still hold the old string.\n\nIn the code: word.upper() returns HI but word is still hi. nums.append(3) changes the same list. add_end appends x onto that same nums, so the last print shows the extra x.\n\nA common mistake is thinking a function cannot change your list. It can, if it mutates the object you passed.", code: "word = \"hi\"\nprint(word.upper())   # new string \"HI\"\nprint(word)           # still \"hi\"\n\nnums = [1, 2]\nnums.append(3)        # same list object changed\nprint(nums)\n\ndef add_end(items):\n    items.append(\"x\")\nadd_end(nums)\nprint(nums)" },
    { id: 42, level: "intermediate", q: "*args and **kwargs?", a: "*args gathers leftover positional values into a tuple. **kwargs gathers leftover named values into a dict. They are useful when you wrap another function or accept a flexible API.\n\nYou do not need them in every function. *nums in add means any number of positional numbers. Loop the tuple to use them.\n\nIn the code: show(1, 2, city=\"Pune\") prints args (1, 2) and kwargs {\"city\": \"Pune\"}. add(1, 2, 3) loops those numbers and returns 6.\n\nA common mistake is mixing up * and **. * is extra unnamed values. ** is extra name=value pairs.", code: "def show(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(\"args\", args)\n    print(\"kwargs\", kwargs)\n\nshow(1, 2, city=\"Pune\")\n\ndef add(*nums):\n    total = 0\n    for n in nums:\n        total += n\n    return total\nprint(add(1, 2, 3))" },
    { id: 43, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a generator?", a: "A generator is a function that uses yield. It gives one item at a time instead of building a huge list. You loop it with for, just like a list.\n\nIt is lazy: the next value is made when you ask. Use it for large streams of data. list(generator) would collect everything if you really need a list.\n\nIn the code: count_to yields 1, then 2, then 3. The for loop prints each value as it arrives. The comment shows list(count_to(3)) would be [1, 2, 3].\n\nA common mistake is calling the generator twice expecting two full lists. Each call is a new walk; you cannot rewind without calling again.", code: "def count_to(n):\n    i = 1\n    while i <= n:\n        yield i          # pause and give i\n        i += 1\n\nfor x in count_to(3):\n    print(x)\n\n# list(count_to(3)) would collect [1, 2, 3]" },
    { id: 44, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a decorator?", a: "A decorator is a function that wraps another function. The @name on the line above def applies that wrap. The wrapper can run code before and after the real function.\n\nFlask and FastAPI use decorators for routes. You can still call the function the same way. The wrapper is what actually runs.\n\nIn the code: loud returns wrapper, which prints start, calls fn, then prints end. @loud on hello means hello() is really wrapper(). The output is start, hi, end.\n\nA common mistake is forgetting to return wrapper from the decorator. Then @loud would replace hello with None.", code: "def loud(fn):\n    def wrapper():\n        print(\"start\")\n        fn()\n        print(\"end\")\n    return wrapper\n\n@loud\ndef hello():\n    print(\"hi\")\n\nhello()" },
    { id: 45, level: "intermediate", q: "pass, break, continue?", a: "pass means do nothing. It is a legal empty body. break leaves the loop right now. continue skips the rest of this round and goes to the next item.\n\nThey keep loops readable. Use them on purpose, not everywhere. An empty def needs pass or a docstring or a real body.\n\nIn the code: n runs 1, 2, 3, 4. continue skips printing 2. break stops before printing 4. So the loop prints 1 and 3. todo() is a stub with pass.\n\nA common mistake is using break when you meant return, or continue when you still needed to update a counter.", code: "for n in [1, 2, 3, 4]:\n    if n == 2:\n        continue      # skip 2\n    if n == 4:\n        break         # stop before printing 4\n    print(n)\n\ndef todo():\n    pass              # empty body is legal" },
    { id: 46, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you copy a list?", a: "b = a does not copy. Both names point at the same list. a.copy() or list(a) or a[:] makes a new outer list. Shallow means inner lists are still shared.\n\ncopy.deepcopy clones nested lists too. Pick a real copy when you will mutate one side. Pick sharing only when you want both names to see the same changes.\n\nIn the code: b is a, so b.append(4) also changes a. c is a.copy(), so c stays [1, 2, 3] after that append.\n\nA common mistake is thinking = copies. It only sticks a second label on the same object.", code: "a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a          # same list\nc = a.copy()   # new outer list\n\nb.append(4)\nprint(a)       # [1, 2, 3, 4] changed\nprint(c)       # [1, 2, 3] safe" },
    { id: 47, level: "intermediate", q: "shallow vs deep copy?", a: "A shallow copy copies the outer box only. Inner objects are still the same objects. A deep copy walks inside and clones nested lists and dicts.\n\nIf you only have numbers in a list, shallow is enough. If you have a list of lists, shallow can surprise you. deepcopy is slower and usually what you want for nested structures.\n\nIn the code: outer is [[1], [2]]. shallow = outer.copy(). deep = copy.deepcopy(outer). shallow[0].append(9) also changes outer[0]. deep stays [[1], [2]].\n\nA common mistake is using .copy() on nested data and believing the inners are safe.", code: "import copy\n\nouter = [[1], [2]]\nshallow = outer.copy()\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(outer)\n\nshallow[0].append(9)\nprint(outer)    # inner list changed\nprint(deep)     # still [[1], [2]]" },
    { id: 48, level: "intermediate", q: "What is PEP 8?", a: "PEP 8 is the common style guide for Python. It says use 4 spaces, snake_case names, and readable line length. Style is not the compiler; it is kindness to the next reader.\n\nTeams follow it so files look like one person wrote them. Tools can hint at many rules. You do not need to memorize every line of the document.\n\nIn the code: user_name and max_retries are snake_case. add_one is a short function with a clear name. print(add_one(max_retries)) shows 4.\n\nA common mistake is mixing camelCase from JavaScript into Python names. Stick to snake_case in Python.", code: "# pep8-ish names: snake_case\nuser_name = \"Ada\"\nmax_retries = 3\n\ndef add_one(value):\n    return value + 1\n\nprint(add_one(max_retries))" },
    { id: 49, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a docstring?", a: "A docstring is the first string inside a function, class, or module. You write it with triple quotes. help() and many editors show it.\n\nIt explains what the function does, not how every line works. Keep it short and honest. __doc__ holds that string on the function object.\n\nIn the code: add's docstring is Return a plus b. print(add.__doc__) shows that text. add(2, 3) still returns 5.\n\nA common mistake is writing a docstring that is just the function name again. Say what comes in and what comes out.", code: "def add(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Return a plus b.\"\"\"\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add.__doc__)\nprint(add(2, 3))" },
    { id: 50, level: "intermediate", q: "main guard?", a: "When you start a file directly, __name__ is the text __main__. When another file imports it, __name__ is the module name. The guard runs demo code only in the first case.\n\nThat way import does not launch your whole script. Put start-up calls inside the guard. Keep functions above the guard so they stay importable.\n\nIn the code: greet returns Hi plus a name. The if block prints greet(\"Ada\") only when this file is the program you started. The comment says another file can import greet without printing.\n\nA common mistake is putting print and work at the top level. Then every import runs that work.", code: "def greet(name):\n    return \"Hi \" + name\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # runs when this file is the program you started\n    print(greet(\"Ada\"))\n\n# another file can: from this_file import greet\n# and that import will not print by itself" },
    { id: 51, level: "intermediate", q: "What is __name__?", a: "__name__ is a string Python sets for every module. Run the file: it is __main__. Import the file: it is the module's name.\n\nThe main guard uses this fact. You rarely set __name__ yourself. Functions inside the file still see the same __name__ as the module.\n\nIn the code: the first print shows __name__, which is __main__ if you started this file. who() returns __name__ too, so the second print matches.\n\nA common mistake is thinking __name__ is the file path. It is a short module name, or __main__.", code: "print(__name__)   # '__main__' if you started this file\n\ndef who():\n    return __name__\n\nprint(who())" },
    { id: 52, level: "beginner", q: "True and False?", a: "True and False are the two boolean values. In an if, some other values also count as false: 0, '', [], {}, None. Non-empty values count as true.\n\nA trap: if marks: is false when marks is 0, even though 0 is a real score. Be explicit with == 0 when zero is a valid number. not flips a boolean.\n\nIn the code: ok is True, so not ok is False. if [] takes the else path and prints empty list is false. marks == 0 treats zero as a real score.\n\nA common mistake is if marks: to test \"did we get a score\". A score of 0 would look missing.", code: "ok = True\nprint(not ok)          # False\n\nif []:\n    print(\"empty list is true\")\nelse:\n    print(\"empty list is false\")\n\nmarks = 0\nif marks == 0:\n    print(\"zero is a real score\")" },
    { id: 53, level: "beginner", q: "How do you check a type?", a: "isinstance(x, int) is the usual way to test a type. It is kinder than type(x) is int because subclasses still pass. type(x) is still useful when you want to print the type.\n\nBeginners can start with isinstance. Do not over-check types if a simple try/except is clearer. isinstance can take a tuple of types.\n\nIn the code: x is 21, so isinstance(x, int) is True. The string and list checks also print True. type(x) prints the int type object.\n\nA common mistake is type(x) == int for values that should also accept bool or a subclass. isinstance handles that better.", code: "x = 21\nprint(isinstance(x, int))\nprint(isinstance(\"hi\", str))\nprint(isinstance([1], list))\n\nprint(type(x))" },
    { id: 54, level: "intermediate", q: "What is zip?", a: "zip walks two or more lists at the same time. It pairs item 0 with item 0, item 1 with item 1, and so on. It stops at the shorter list.\n\nThis is how you combine names with ages without index math. Wrap with list() if you want to print all pairs at once. The result of zip is lazy until you loop it.\n\nIn the code: names is Ada, Grace and ages is 20, 21. The for loop prints Ada 20 then Grace 21. list(zip(...)) is a list of tuples.\n\nA common mistake is assuming zip pads the shorter list. Extra items on the long side are dropped.", code: "names = [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\nages = [20, 21]\n\nfor name, age in zip(names, ages):\n    print(name, age)\n\nprint(list(zip(names, ages)))" },
    { id: 55, level: "intermediate", q: "map and filter?", a: "map runs a function on every item. filter keeps items where the function is true. In modern Python, a list comprehension is often easier to read.\n\nYou should still recognize map and filter in older code. Both return lazy views, so list() materializes them. Prefer the form your teammates can read fastest.\n\nIn the code: map doubles each number to [2, 4, 6, 8]. filter keeps evens [2, 4]. The last print shows the same doubling as a comprehension.\n\nA common mistake is forgetting list() around map, then printing a map object instead of the numbers.", code: "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\n\ndoubled = list(map(lambda n: n * 2, nums))\nevens = list(filter(lambda n: n % 2 == 0, nums))\nprint(doubled)\nprint(evens)\n\n# same idea, often clearer\nprint([n * 2 for n in nums])" },
    { id: 56, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you read a CSV?", a: "CSV is a text table with commas between fields. csv.DictReader turns each row into a dict using the header line. pandas.read_csv is common in data work, but csv is enough to learn the idea.\n\nEach row is one record. Each column is a field. You loop rows; you do not need a spreadsheet app.\n\nIn the code: raw is a tiny CSV in a string, with a header and two people. StringIO makes that string look like a file. DictReader lets you print row[\"name\"] and row[\"age\"] for Ada then Lin.\n\nA common mistake is splitting on commas by hand and breaking when a field contains a comma. Use the csv module.", code: "import csv\nfrom io import StringIO\n\nraw = \"name,age\\nAda,21\\nLin,18\"\nreader = csv.DictReader(StringIO(raw))\nfor row in reader:\n    print(row[\"name\"], row[\"age\"])" },
    { id: 57, level: "intermediate", q: "json in Python?", a: "JSON is text that looks a bit like a dict. json.dumps turns a dict or list into text. json.loads turns text back into Python objects. load and dump work with files.\n\nAPIs almost always send JSON. After loads, you read keys like a normal dict. dumps is for sending; loads is for receiving.\n\nIn the code: user is a dict. dumps makes a JSON string. loads parses that string back. back[\"name\"] is Ada.\n\nA common mistake is swapping dumps and loads. dumps needs an object. loads needs a string.", code: "import json\n\nuser = {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"age\": 21}\ntext = json.dumps(user)\nprint(text)\n\nback = json.loads(text)\nprint(back[\"name\"])" },
    { id: 58, level: "intermediate", q: "What is datetime?", a: "datetime, date, and timedelta live in the datetime module. You can take a date, add days, or format a string. For apps, UTC is a safer default than local time.\n\nDo not store dates as random strings if you can help it. strftime turns a date into text with a pattern. timedelta is a duration you can add or subtract.\n\nIn the code: today is 1 Sep 2026. tomorrow adds one day. print shows both dates. strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") prints 2026-09-01.\n\nA common mistake is adding 1 to a date as if it were an int. Use timedelta.", code: "from datetime import date, timedelta\n\ntoday = date(2026, 9, 1)\ntomorrow = today + timedelta(days=1)\nprint(today)\nprint(tomorrow)\nprint(today.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\"))" },
    { id: 59, level: "intermediate", q: "random module?", a: "random picks fake-random numbers and items. choice picks one item from a list. randint picks a whole number in a range. random() picks a float from 0.0 up to 1.0.\n\nFor passwords or security tokens, use the secrets module instead. seed makes the sequence repeat, which is good for teaching and tests. Games and demos are the right place for random.\n\nIn the code: seed(1) locks the sequence. choice picks from a, b, c. randint picks 1 to 6. random() prints a float.\n\nA common mistake is using random for security codes. Attackers can predict that kind of randomness.", code: "import random\n\nrandom.seed(1)   # same results every run, good for teaching\nprint(random.choice([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]))\nprint(random.randint(1, 6))\nprint(random.random())   # 0.0 to 1.0" },
    { id: 60, level: "intermediate", q: "What is pip vs conda?", a: "pip installs Python packages from PyPI. conda can also install Python itself and many science tools. A beginner can start with pip plus a venv.\n\nconda shines when you need a whole data stack in one go. Your .py code still uses import either way. The installer is not the language.\n\nIn the code: math and json are imported the same way no matter which tool installed extra packages. sqrt(9) is 3. dumps prints a small JSON object.\n\nA common mistake is mixing pip installs into a conda env without a plan, then getting two copies of a library.", code: "# both tools end with the same idea: import the library\nimport math\nimport json\n\nprint(math.sqrt(9))\nprint(json.dumps({\"via\": \"import\"}))" },
    { id: 61, level: "beginner", q: "How do you install Python packages for a project?", a: "Give the project its own environment so packages stay local. Then install the library name you need. Then write import in your .py file.\n\nAvoid dumping everything into the global Python if you can. The code you read in a file is the import side. The install step happens once in that environment.\n\nIn the code: json is imported after it exists in the env. dumps prints the config dict as text. The comment reminds you the file still looks like import plus use.\n\nA common mistake is installing a package and then running a different Python that cannot see it.", code: "# after a package exists in your project env, you import it\nimport json\n\nconfig = {\"debug\": True}\nprint(json.dumps(config))\n\n# your file still looks the same: import the name, then use it" },
    { id: 62, level: "intermediate", q: "What is an iterator?", a: "An iterator is something a for loop can walk. It has __iter__ and __next__ under the hood. A list is iterable: you can get a new iterator from it.\n\nA file object is already an iterator of lines. You rarely call __next__ yourself; for does it. When next runs out, it raises StopIteration and the loop ends.\n\nIn the code: iter(nums) makes an iterator. next(it) is 10, then 20. The for loop starts a fresh iterator over nums and prints 10, 20, 30.\n\nA common mistake is calling next on the list itself. You need iter(list) first, or just use for.", code: "nums = [10, 20, 30]\nit = iter(nums)\n\nprint(next(it))   # 10\nprint(next(it))   # 20\n\nfor n in nums:    # for uses an iterator\n    print(n)" },
    { id: 63, level: "intermediate", q: "with statement?", a: "with calls enter at the start and exit at the end. That is how files close and locks release. You get cleanup even if an error happens in the middle.\n\nopen is the example everyone learns first. Later you will see with around databases too. closing() turns a close() object into a context manager.\n\nIn the code: StringIO holds two lines. with closing(buffer) as f reads the whole buffer and still closes it. The comment shows the everyday with open pattern.\n\nA common mistake is using with on an object that is not a context manager. You need __enter__/__exit__ or a helper like closing.", code: "from contextlib import closing\nfrom io import StringIO\n\nbuffer = StringIO(\"line1\\nline2\\n\")\nwith closing(buffer) as f:\n    print(f.read())\n\n# with open(\"a.txt\") as f:\n#     text = f.read()" },
    { id: 64, level: "intermediate", q: "raise an error?", a: "raise stops the function and signals a problem. ValueError is a good type when the value is wrong. The message should help a human fix the input.\n\nCallers can except that error if they want to recover. Do not raise if you can return a normal value instead. Raise early at the edge of your function.\n\nIn the code: set_marks(80) is valid and prints 80. set_marks(200) raises ValueError. The except prints marks must be 0 to 100.\n\nA common mistake is raising a bare Exception with no message. Then the caller cannot tell what was wrong.", code: "def set_marks(marks):\n    if marks < 0 or marks > 100:\n        raise ValueError(\"marks must be 0 to 100\")\n    return marks\n\nprint(set_marks(80))\ntry:\n    set_marks(200)\nexcept ValueError as err:\n    print(err)" },
    { id: 65, level: "intermediate", q: "assert?", a: "assert checks a condition that should always be true. If it is false, Python raises AssertionError. It is for internal this cannot happen checks.\n\nDo not use assert as your only check of user input, because it can be turned off with python -O. raise ValueError is clearer for bad user data. The message after the comma is the error text.\n\nIn the code: area(3) returns 9. area(-1) fails the assert. The except prints width must be positive.\n\nA common mistake is validating user forms with assert. Use raise ValueError for that.", code: "def area(width):\n    assert width > 0, \"width must be positive\"\n    return width * width\n\nprint(area(3))\ntry:\n    area(-1)\nexcept AssertionError as err:\n    print(err)" },
    { id: 66, level: "advanced", q: "GIL?", a: "The GIL means one thread runs Python bytecode at a time. Threads still help when you wait on the network or disk. Heavy number crunching in pure Python does not get faster with more threads.\n\nPeople use multiprocessing or libraries written in C for CPU work. Two threads can still interleave on shared data. You still need locks if you mutate shared objects.\n\nIn the code: box holds n. bump adds 1 a thousand times. Two Thread objects share the same dict. The comment notes the GIL still allows interleaving of those += steps.\n\nA common mistake is expecting two threads to double the speed of a pure Python math loop. The GIL usually blocks that.", code: "import threading\n\nbox = {\"n\": 0}\n\ndef bump():\n    for _ in range(1000):\n        box[\"n\"] += 1\n\nt1 = threading.Thread(target=bump)\nt2 = threading.Thread(target=bump)\n# two threads share the same dict; GIL still allows interleaving" },
    { id: 67, level: "advanced", q: "list vs generator memory?", a: "A list holds every item at once. A generator holds the recipe for the next item. For a huge file, loop lines; do not build a giant list of all lines if you do not need it.\n\nIf you need random access, you still want a list. Pick based on size and how you walk the data. sum() can consume a generator without storing every number.\n\nIn the code: huge_list builds a full list in memory. huge_gen yields one i at a time. sum(huge_gen(5)) is 0+1+2+3+4 without keeping the whole list.\n\nA common mistake is list(huge_gen(n)) for a massive n, which throws away the memory benefit.", code: "def huge_list(n):\n    return [i for i in range(n)]   # all in memory\n\ndef huge_gen(n):\n    i = 0\n    while i < n:\n        yield i\n        i += 1\n\nprint(sum(huge_gen(5)))   # 0+1+2+3+4" },
    { id: 68, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you write a simple test?", a: "A test is a function that compares a real answer to the expected one. assert add(2, 3) == 5 is a tiny test. pytest runs functions whose names start with test_.\n\nOne test should check one idea. If the function changes by mistake, the test fails. Calling the test function yourself also works in a tiny file.\n\nIn the code: add returns a + b. test_add asserts 2+3 is 5 and 0+0 is 0. test_add() runs those checks, then tests passed prints.\n\nA common mistake is writing tests that print instead of assert. Then failures stay quiet.", code: "def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\ndef test_add():\n    assert add(2, 3) == 5\n    assert add(0, 0) == 0\n\ntest_add()\nprint(\"tests passed\")" },
    { id: 69, level: "beginner", q: "What is pytest?", a: "pytest is a popular tool that finds and runs tests. Files are often named test_something.py. Functions are named test_something.\n\nYou do not need a class to start. Your job is to write clear assert lines. pytest reports which assert failed and with what values.\n\nIn the code: greet builds Hi plus a name. test_greet asserts greet(\"Ada\") == \"Hi Ada\". Calling test_greet() then printing ok mimics a passing pytest run.\n\nA common mistake is naming the file tests.py without test_ functions. pytest will not collect them.", code: "# a pytest-style test is just a function with assert\ndef greet(name):\n    return \"Hi \" + name\n\ndef test_greet():\n    assert greet(\"Ada\") == \"Hi Ada\"\n\ntest_greet()\nprint(\"ok\")" },
    { id: 70, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a package vs a module?", a: "A module is one .py file. A package is a folder of modules, often with __init__.py. You import a module as import math. You import from a package as import mypkg.util.\n\nPackages help when a project grows past one file. math and json in the snippet are modules from the standard library. The idea is the same for your own folder of files.\n\nIn the code: import math then math.sqrt(9) prints 3. import json then dumps prints a small object. Each import is one module unit.\n\nA common mistake is naming your file json.py and then hiding the real json module.", code: "# math is a module (one library unit)\nimport math\nprint(math.sqrt(9))\n\n# json is also a module\nimport json\nprint(json.dumps({\"ok\": True}))" },
    { id: 71, level: "intermediate", q: "sys.argv?", a: "sys.argv is a list of words from the command line. Index 0 is the script name. Index 1 is the first word you passed.\n\nargparse is nicer when you have flags. In teaching code we just read a list that looks like argv. Always check the length before you read argv[1].\n\nIn the code: argv pretends the program started as app.py hello. [0] is app.py. [1] is hello. Real programs read sys.argv the same way.\n\nA common mistake is using argv[1] when the user passed no extra word. That raises IndexError.", code: "import sys\n\n# teaching: pretend the program was started with extra words\nargv = [\"app.py\", \"hello\"]\nprint(argv[0])    # script name\nprint(argv[1])    # first argument\n\n# real programs read sys.argv the same way" },
    { id: 72, level: "beginner", q: "How do you run a Python file?", a: "A .py file is just text until the Python program reads it. The first lines run from top to bottom. A good file has a small starting point, like a main function.\n\nThe main guard keeps import from launching the program. Keep a tiny hello world while you learn. Read those first lines to understand the file.\n\nIn the code: main prints Hello from hello.py. The if __name__ == \"__main__\" block calls main() only when you started this file.\n\nA common mistake is putting all work at the top with no main. Then testing one function also runs the whole script.", code: "def main():\n    print(\"Hello from hello.py\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()" },
    { id: 73, level: "intermediate", q: "What is slicing copies?", a: "b = a[:] makes a new outer list. If the items are numbers or strings, changing b[0] does not change a[0]. If the items are lists, those inner lists are still shared.\n\nThat is the shallow copy rule again. Replacing a top-level item is fine. Mutating a shared inner list is not.\n\nIn the code: b[0] = 9 leaves a as [1, 2, 3]. nested is two inner lists. c = nested[:] shares those inners, so c[0].append(8) also changes nested.\n\nA common mistake is using [:] on nested data and believing it is a full clone.", code: "a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a[:]\nb[0] = 9\nprint(a)          # [1, 2, 3]\n\nnested = [[1], [2]]\nc = nested[:]\nc[0].append(8)\nprint(nested)     # inner list changed" },
    { id: 74, level: "beginner", q: "How do you swap two variables?", a: "Python lets you write a, b = b, a. You do not need a temp box. The right side is packed, then unpacked into the left.\n\nThis also works for more than two names. It is a famous tiny Python trick. Both names swap in one step.\n\nIn the code: a starts 1 and b starts 2, then they swap to 2 1. x, y, z rotate so z moves to x, x to y, and y to z.\n\nA common mistake is writing a = b then b = a. That copies b into both names and loses the old a.", code: "a = 1\nb = 2\na, b = b, a\nprint(a, b)       # 2 1\n\nx, y, z = 10, 20, 30\nx, y, z = z, x, y\nprint(x, y, z)" },
    { id: 75, level: "intermediate", q: "enumerate?", a: "enumerate pairs a count with each item. start=1 makes the count begin at 1 instead of 0. It is cleaner than i = 0 and i += 1.\n\nUse it when you need both the item and its place. The loop still looks like a normal for. Unpacking i, fruit is the usual style.\n\nIn the code: the first loop prints 0 apple and 1 mango. The second loop uses start=1 so it prints 1 apple and 2 mango.\n\nA common mistake is enumerate(fruits)[i] as if it were a list. Loop it, or wrap list() if you truly need indexes later.", code: "fruits = [\"apple\", \"mango\"]\n\nfor i, fruit in enumerate(fruits):\n    print(i, fruit)\n\nfor i, fruit in enumerate(fruits, start=1):\n    print(i, fruit)" },
    { id: 76, level: "beginner", q: "How do you check if a key is in a dict?", a: "Write \"name\" in user to test for a key. That is True or False and does not crash. Do not use if user[\"name\"] only to test, because a missing key explodes.\n\nYou can combine this with get. in looks at keys, not values. not in is the opposite check.\n\nIn the code: name is in user so the first print is True. email is missing so False. The if prints no email key.\n\nA common mistake is writing if \"Ada\" in user to find a value. in on a dict checks keys, so that is False.", code: "user = {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\n\nprint(\"name\" in user)     # True\nprint(\"email\" in user)    # False\n\nif \"email\" not in user:\n    print(\"no email key\")" },
    { id: 77, level: "beginner", q: "How do you check if an item is in a list?", a: "Write if \"apple\" in fruits to test membership in a list. Python scans the list until it finds the item. If you check many times, a set is faster.\n\nin is readable and enough for small lists. It is True or False, not the index. Use .index() if you need the position and the item exists.\n\nIn the code: apple is in fruits, kiwi is not. fast is a set of the same names, so mango in fast is still True.\n\nA common mistake is using in on a list of dicts expecting to find a field. It checks whole items, not fields inside.", code: "fruits = [\"apple\", \"mango\"]\nprint(\"apple\" in fruits)\nprint(\"kiwi\" in fruits)\n\nfast = set(fruits)\nprint(\"mango\" in fast)    # still True, faster for big sets" },
    { id: 78, level: "intermediate", q: "What is * in unpacking?", a: "first, *rest = list puts the leftover items into rest. You can also pass a list as separate arguments with add(*nums). Here * is packing and unpacking, not multiplication.\n\nRead it as the rest of the items. This keeps function calls flexible. rest is a list of what was left.\n\nIn the code: first is 1 and rest is [2, 3, 4]. add needs three arguments. add(*nums) unpacks [1, 2, 3] into 1, 2, 3 and prints 6.\n\nA common mistake is add(nums) without *. Then a is the whole list and b and c are missing.", code: "first, *rest = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(first)   # 1\nprint(rest)    # [2, 3, 4]\n\ndef add(a, b, c):\n    return a + b + c\n\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(add(*nums))" },
    { id: 79, level: "intermediate", q: "dataclass?", a: "A dataclass is a decorator that writes a simple __init__ for you. You list fields with types. It is great for objects that mainly hold data.\n\nYou can still add methods. Use a normal class if you need heavy custom init logic. print of a dataclass shows the fields by default.\n\nIn the code: User has name and age. User(\"Ada\", 21) fills both fields. print shows Ada 21 and the dataclass repr.\n\nA common mistake is mutating a dataclass field and expecting it to be frozen. Frozen is an extra option you must turn on.", code: "from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass User:\n    name: str\n    age: int\n\nu = User(\"Ada\", 21)\nprint(u.name, u.age)\nprint(u)" },
    { id: 80, level: "intermediate", q: "type hints?", a: "Type hints are optional notes like a: int and -> int. Python does not enforce them at runtime by itself. Tools such as mypy can check them.\n\nThey help readers and editors. Start with hints on public functions. They are not a replacement for real checks on user input.\n\nIn the code: add takes two ints and returns an int. greet takes a str and returns a str. The calls still run like normal Python and print 5 and Hi Ada.\n\nA common mistake is thinking add(\"2\", \"3\") will be blocked at runtime. Hints do not stop that unless you add a checker.", code: "def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a + b\n\ndef greet(name: str) -> str:\n    return \"Hi \" + name\n\nprint(add(2, 3))\nprint(greet(\"Ada\"))" },
    { id: 81, level: "beginner", q: "How do you make an empty list / dict / set?", a: "[] is an empty list. {} is an empty dict, not a set. set() is an empty set. This mix-up is very common in interviews.\n\nRemember {} already means dict in Python history. type() prints which constructor you used. Fill them later with append, key assignment, or add.\n\nIn the code: three empty boxes are made. type prints list, dict, and set in that order.\n\nA common mistake is empty_set = {} and then calling .add. dict has no add.", code: "empty_list = []\nempty_dict = {}\nempty_set = set()\n\nprint(type(empty_list))\nprint(type(empty_dict))\nprint(type(empty_set))" },
    { id: 82, level: "intermediate", q: "Why is {} not an empty set?", a: "{} was already used for an empty dictionary. The language cannot give the same spelling two meanings. So an empty set must be set().\n\nA non-empty set can still use {1, 2}. Once you see it, you never forget it. type() is the quick proof.\n\nIn the code: type({}) is dict. type(set()) is set. type({1, 2}) is set. {1, 2, 2} collapses to unique items.\n\nA common mistake is writing if x == {} to mean empty set. That only matches an empty dict.", code: "print(type({}))       # dict\nprint(type(set()))    # set\nprint(type({1, 2}))   # set with items\n\nboth = {1, 2, 2}\nprint(both)" },
    { id: 83, level: "intermediate", q: "is vs == for None?", a: "Write x is None or x is not None. PEP 8 asks for this. is checks the single None object.\n\n== None usually works but is not the usual style. 0 is not None. Empty string is not None either.\n\nIn the code: x is None prints True, then is not None prints False. y is 0, so y is None is False.\n\nA common mistake is if not x to mean is None. That also treats 0, '', and [] as missing.", code: "x = None\nprint(x is None)\nprint(x is not None)\n\ny = 0\nprint(y is None)      # False, 0 is not None" },
    { id: 84, level: "advanced", q: "What are dunder methods?", a: "Dunder means double underscore. Names like __init__ hook your class into Python syntax. __init__ builds the object. __str__ is used by print. __len__ is used by len().\n\nYou only write the ones you need. They let your objects behave like built-in types. The names are fixed; you do not invent random __foo__.\n\nIn the code: User stores name in __init__. __str__ returns that name. print(u) therefore shows Ada instead of an ugly default object line.\n\nA common mistake is writing __str__ but returning nothing. Then print shows None.", code: "class User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.name   # used by print(user)\n\nu = User(\"Ada\")\nprint(u)" },
    { id: 85, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you format numbers?", a: "f\"{price:.2f}\" is two decimal places. f\"{n:,}\" adds thousands separators. round(n, 2) returns a number, not always perfect money math.\n\nFormatting is for display; rounding is for a number. Keep money rules extra careful in real apps. The format spec lives inside the braces after a colon.\n\nIn the code: 19.5 prints as 19.50. 1000000 prints as 1,000,000. round(2.567, 2) prints 2.57.\n\nA common mistake is using round for currency and summing many rounded pennies. Display with format; compute with care.", code: "price = 19.5\nprint(f\"{price:.2f}\")     # 19.50\n\nn = 1000000\nprint(f\"{n:,}\")           # 1,000,000\n\nprint(round(2.567, 2))" },
    { id: 86, level: "beginner", q: "How do you get the length of a string?", a: "len(\"hello\") is 5. You can also read [0] and slices, like a list of letters. Strings cannot be changed in place.\n\nYou make a new string instead. len also works on lists and dicts. Index errors happen if you ask for a character that is not there.\n\nIn the code: word is hello. len is 5. [0] is h. [-1] is o. [1:4] is ell.\n\nA common mistake is word[5] on a 5-letter word. Indexes only go 0 to 4.", code: "word = \"hello\"\nprint(len(word))\nprint(word[0])\nprint(word[-1])\nprint(word[1:4])     # ell" },
    { id: 87, level: "intermediate", q: "string is immutable — meaning?", a: "Methods like upper() return a new string. The old string stays the same unless you assign back. There is no word[0] = \"H\".\n\nThis makes strings safe to share. name = name.upper() is the usual pattern. Immutable means you replace, you do not edit.\n\nIn the code: name.upper() prints ADA but name is still ada. After name = name.upper(), name is ADA.\n\nA common mistake is calling name.upper() and expecting name to change. You must assign the result.", code: "name = \"ada\"\nprint(name.upper())   # ADA\nprint(name)           # still ada\n\nname = name.upper()\nprint(name)           # ADA" },
    { id: 88, level: "intermediate", q: "pathlib?", a: "Path is a modern object for file paths. You join folders with / instead of messy string glue. It works well on Windows and Linux.\n\nNew code often prefers Path over os.path. You still open files the same idea: read and write text. suffix is the extension. name is the final piece.\n\nIn the code: folder is Path(\"data\"). / \"file.txt\" builds data/file.txt (or data\\file.txt on Windows, shown as a Path). suffix is .txt and name is file.txt.\n\nA common mistake is joining paths with + and missing a slash. Use / on Path objects.", code: "from pathlib import Path\n\nfolder = Path(\"data\")\nfile_path = folder / \"file.txt\"\nprint(file_path)\n\nprint(file_path.suffix)    # .txt\nprint(file_path.name)      # file.txt" },
    { id: 89, level: "beginner", q: "How do you install and import requests?", a: "After the library exists in your environment, you import requests. .get(url) returns a response object. You should check whether the call worked before you trust the body.\n\nThis file teaches the import and the response object, not a shopping command. Always handle a failed response. .json() parses a JSON body.\n\nIn the code: Response is a stand-in with ok True and json() returning {\"name\": \"Ada\"}. If ok, the code reads data[\"name\"] and prints Ada.\n\nA common mistake is calling .json() without checking ok, then crashing on an HTML error page.", code: "# teaching the requests shape with a stand-in object\nclass Response:\n    ok = True\n    def json(self):\n        return {\"name\": \"Ada\"}\n\nresponse = Response()\nif response.ok:\n    data = response.json()\n    print(data[\"name\"])" },
    { id: 90, level: "intermediate", q: "What is JSON from an API in Python?", a: "Many APIs send JSON text. response.json() parses it. You usually get a dict or a list. Then you read keys like a normal dict.\n\nCheck ok first so you do not parse an error page. get is safer than square brackets for optional fields. The snippet shows the dict you would have after parse.\n\nIn the code: payload is already the parsed dict. data[\"name\"] is Ada. data.get(\"email\") is None because email is missing.\n\nA common mistake is treating the JSON text as a dict before you parse it. A string has no [\"name\"] key that means Ada.", code: "payload = {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"id\": 1}\n\n# what response.json() gives you\ndata = payload\nprint(data[\"name\"])\nprint(data.get(\"email\"))" },
    { id: 91, level: "advanced", q: "pass by object reference?", a: "Python passes the object, not a full copy. If the object is mutable and you append, the caller sees it. If you rebind the name items = [\"new\"], the caller's name does not change.\n\nMutating is sharing. Rebinding is a new sticker on a local name. Interviews like this distinction.\n\nIn the code: append_x mutates nums to [1, \"x\"]. rebind only changes its local items name, so nums stays [1, \"x\"] after rebind.\n\nA common mistake is saying Python is pass by value or pass by reference as if those phrases were enough. Say mutate versus rebind.", code: "def append_x(items):\n    items.append(\"x\")     # mutates the same list\n\ndef rebind(items):\n    items = [\"new\"]       # only this name changes\n\nnums = [1]\nappend_x(nums)\nprint(nums)               # [1, \"x\"]\nrebind(nums)\nprint(nums)               # still [1, \"x\"]" },
    { id: 92, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you flatten a simple list of lists?", a: "A double loop unrolls nested lists one level. A double list comprehension does the same. This is not a deep flatten of lists inside lists inside lists.\n\nStart with the loop if the comprehension feels noisy. Same result either way. Append each inner item onto a new flat list.\n\nIn the code: rows is [1, 2] and [3, 4]. The nested for builds [1, 2, 3, 4]. The comprehension prints the same list.\n\nA common mistake is summing the inner lists with + in a way that still keeps one level of nesting, or flattening too deep.", code: "rows = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\n\nflat = []\nfor row in rows:\n    for item in row:\n        flat.append(item)\nprint(flat)\n\nprint([item for row in rows for item in row])" },
    { id: 93, level: "beginner", q: "What does import * do and why avoid it?", a: "from math import * dumps many names into your file. You cannot see where sqrt came from. Names can silently overwrite each other.\n\nImport the names you use, or import math. Be explicit so future you can read the file. Star imports also make tools worse at finding definitions.\n\nIn the code: only sqrt and pi are imported. print(sqrt(9)) and print(pi) work. The comment forbids import *.\n\nA common mistake is import * in a big file and then wondering which module defined a helper.", code: "from math import sqrt, pi\n\nprint(sqrt(9))\nprint(pi)\n\n# not this: from math import *" },
    { id: 94, level: "intermediate", q: "logging vs print?", a: "print is fine while you learn. logging has levels like info and error. You can send logs to a file or a system later.\n\nBigger programs prefer logging so you can turn volume up or down. Start with print, then learn logging.info. Never log passwords.\n\nIn the code: basicConfig sets INFO level. info logs server started. error logs payment failed. print still shows a learning hello.\n\nA common mistake is logging everything as error, so real errors drown. Use info, warning, and error on purpose.", code: "import logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nlogging.info(\"server started\")\nlogging.error(\"payment failed\")\n\n# print still works for tiny scripts\nprint(\"hello while learning\")" },
    { id: 95, level: "beginner", q: "How do you write a while loop?", a: "while repeats as long as the condition is true. You must change something so it can become false. If you forget, the loop never ends.\n\nfor is better when you already have a list. while is good when you wait for a condition. Indent the body four spaces.\n\nIn the code: n starts at 3. The loop prints 3, 2, 1 while n > 0. n -= 1 is the change that ends the loop. Then lift off prints.\n\nA common mistake is forgetting n -= 1. Then the loop prints 3 forever.", code: "n = 3\nwhile n > 0:\n    print(n)\n    n -= 1          # this must happen\n\nprint(\"lift off\")" },
    { id: 96, level: "intermediate", q: "ternary in Python?", a: "The one-line if expression is value_if_true if condition else value_if_false. It returns a value; it is not a full if block. Keep it short or it becomes hard to read.\n\nUse a normal if when each branch has many lines. People still call this a ternary even though the word order is unusual. Both sides should be simple values.\n\nIn the code: ok is True so msg is yes. marks is 75 so status is pass. print shows both results.\n\nA common mistake is writing if condition then a else b like other languages. Python puts the true value first.", code: "ok = True\nmsg = \"yes\" if ok else \"no\"\nprint(msg)\n\nmarks = 75\nstatus = \"pass\" if marks >= 40 else \"fail\"\nprint(status)" },
    { id: 97, level: "advanced", q: "What is a context manager you might write?", a: "A context manager has __enter__ and __exit__. You can also build one with @contextmanager. Beginners can just use with open.\n\nWriting your own is for cleanup you want to guarantee. __enter__ runs at the start of with. __exit__ runs at the end, even after an error.\n\nIn the code: Quiet prints enter, then the with body prints inside, then __exit__ prints exit. The with block is the inside part.\n\nA common mistake is putting cleanup only after the with body without __exit__. Then an error would skip the cleanup.", code: "class Quiet:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"enter\")\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        print(\"exit\")\n\nwith Quiet():\n    print(\"inside\")" },
    { id: 98, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you measure time of a piece of code?", a: "time.perf_counter() is a good clock for short benchmarks. You save the start, run the code, subtract. time.time() is a simpler wall clock.\n\nDo not time print-heavy demos and call it science. Repeat a few times if the number jumps. The elapsed value is seconds as a float.\n\nIn the code: start is captured, then a loop sums 0 to 999. elapsed is now minus start. print shows the total and the duration.\n\nA common mistake is using datetime.now() differences for tiny snippets. perf_counter is meant for this.", code: "import time\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\ntotal = 0\nfor i in range(1000):\n    total += i\nelapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\nprint(total)\nprint(elapsed)" },
    { id: 99, level: "beginner", q: "What should go in .gitignore for Python?", a: "Skip the virtualenv folder, cache folders, and secret files. __pycache__ is generated junk. .env may hold passwords.\n\nYour source .py files and requirements.txt do belong in Git. The list is the idea as data, not a terminal lesson. Secrets that leak must be rotated.\n\nIn the code: ignore lists .venv/, __pycache__/, .env, and *.pyc. The loop prints do not commit for each line.\n\nA common mistake is committing .env because it was already tracked. Add it to gitignore and untrack it.", code: "ignore = [\n    \".venv/\",\n    \"__pycache__/\",\n    \".env\",\n    \"*.pyc\",\n]\nfor line in ignore:\n    print(\"do not commit\", line)" },
    { id: 100, level: "advanced", q: "How do you talk through a Python interview task?", a: "First restate the input and the output in plain words. Pick a simple structure: list or dict. Write a clear loop before a clever one-liner.\n\nName empty input and None as edge cases. Add a tiny assert if there is time. Calm and clear beats fancy.\n\nIn the code: unique_names documents the goal. Empty rows return []. dict.fromkeys keeps first-seen order. Two asserts lock the examples, then talk, then test prints.\n\nA common mistake is jumping to a one-liner before you can explain empty input. Interviewers listen to the talk.", code: "def unique_names(rows):\n    \"\"\"Return names in first-seen order.\"\"\"\n    if not rows:\n        return []\n    return list(dict.fromkeys(rows))\n\nassert unique_names([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"]) == [\"a\", \"b\"]\nassert unique_names([]) == []\nprint(\"talk, then test\")" }
  ]
};
